-- #1620 — two interfaces in ONE file declare the SAME method name `ping` at
-- DIFFERENT return types, both implemented on one concrete type `T`.
--
-- `check` accepts the program at exit 0, having resolved `ping T` to Beta's
-- method (return type Int — raw-probe.mdk shows this directly: replacing the
-- comparand with a String makes `check` report `Type mismatch: Int vs String`).
-- Both engines then DELIVER Alpha's body: the built binary exits 0 printing the
-- raw machine word of the string "alpha" where an Int belongs, and the
-- interpreter E-PANICs with `intToString: not an Int`.
--
-- HAND-DERIVED EXPECTATION. Which interface the occurrence of `ping` belongs to
-- is a name-resolution question and the answer is genuinely in dispute (cf.
-- #1182), so this file does NOT assert that Beta is the right choice. What is
-- assertable without settling that: A PROGRAM THE COMPILER ACCEPTS MUST DELIVER A
-- VALUE OF THE TYPE IT ACCEPTED IT AT. It was accepted at `ping T : Int` with
-- Beta's body being 7, so `ping T == 7` must be True. Every fix direction drains
-- this row:
--   * resolve consistently to Beta -> this prints True;
--   * resolve to Alpha            -> `ping T : String` and this file no longer
--                                    type-checks, so `medaka build` exits 1;
--   * reject the collision        -> likewise, `medaka build` exits 1.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE PROJECTION AND NOT THE RAW VALUE. The raw form's binary prints a
-- LIVE HEAP ADDRESS — measured three executions, three different values — so an
-- exact-match pin on it would red on the very next run for no reason. See
-- raw-probe.mdk, which is not graded.
interface Alpha a where
  ping : a -> String

interface Beta a where
  ping : a -> Int

data T = T

impl Alpha T where
  ping _ = "alpha"

impl Beta T where
  ping _ = 7

main = println (ping T == 7)
